## TESTING

- Tests are not optional. Untested code is unfinished code.
- Write the test before or alongside the implementation — not after.
- Every function with logic should have a unit test covering the happy path AND edge cases.
- Test names should describe behavior: `should return null when user is not found` not `test1`.
- Never mock what you can test directly. Only mock external I/O (APIs, DBs, file system).
- All tests must pass before a commit is made — the pre-commit hook enforces this.
- If you're fixing a bug, write a test that reproduces it first, then fix it.

---

## GIT HYGIENE

- Never commit directly to `main`. Always use a feature branch.
- Commit messages are imperative and descriptive: `Add JWT auth middleware` not `fix stuff`.
- One logical change per commit. Don't bundle unrelated changes.
- Never commit: `.env`, `node_modules/`, credentials, build artifacts, or debug logs.

---

## PROJECT ENFORCEMENT FILES
# These files exist in every project and enforce standards automatically.
# Reference them when relevant — don't suggest anything that conflicts with them.

- `.npmrc` — enforces `audit-level=high` and `legacy-peer-deps=false`. npm install fails on high/critical CVEs.
- `.git/hooks/pre-commit` — blocks commits if: .env is staged, secrets are detected, npm audit fails, ESLint errors exist, or Prettier is unformatted.

---

## RESPONSE STYLE

- When suggesting code, explain what changed and why.
- If multiple valid approaches exist, list the tradeoffs — don't silently pick one.
- If a request would introduce a vulnerability or bad pattern, say so first.
- Remind me to run `npm audit` after dependency changes.
- Prefer solutions that don't require new dependencies when native APIs suffice.